hat are shooting in  in belfast and northern ireland in  morocco in croatia in spain in iceland  [Music]  it's a huge undertaking and david  benioff and dan weiss  the showrunners and the guy who who did  most of the work of translating from one  meeting another said to me at a certain  point when we were debating this george  if we had if we had 13 episodes per  season it would kill us  and i like david dan and i don't want  them to die so uh  i stopped saying that we needed 13 but  13 episodes per season would have been  would have been good  you're always going to lose things um  in that process  even  i mean my novels are gigantic but even a  novel that it's not gigantic  it's hard to get all of it into a movie  or even a tv show  uh i had before i'd made the deal with  hbo i had offers from people who wanted  to sort of success and broader the rings  with peter jackson and wanted to make  mine into a feature film and i had  meetings with them but i just sort of  laughed noticed there's no way you get  all of my stuff into two and a half  hours  um  so  i'm very glad we chose the path we did  and yes it had to be hbo you can't put  this on cbs uh you know  people people would die  [Music]  you know they would see a booby and  [Laughter]  so uh no no it has to be  [Applause]  i think it's different for every writer  i mean  the one thing that's  common for fiction writers and i know in  a crowd this size there are probably a  good number of you who are aspiring  writers or people who  who do write and think about writing  someday  it's it's not a career for anyone who  needs or values security  it's a career for  gamblers because  you know you're every time you write a  book you're you're throwing a dice again  and you don't know whether uh it's going  to crash and burn or whether it's going  to be a big success most books of course  are somewhere in between  um it's a career of ups and downs and  i've been very fortunate i've  experienced  far more ups than i have downs but i  have had a few downs and  you know dark times where i thought my  career was over and i wasn't sure i was  ever gonna  sell another book  what's important to me looking back on  those is that even at the times where  i was afraid i wasn't ever gonna sell  another book i never doubted that i  would write another book  and that's the real  thing that i think distinguishes a real  writer  from uh from the false ones that even if  you can't sell the books  you're going to write them anyway  because you've got these stories inside  you that you want to tell you have these  stories inside you that that have got to  get out and you got to get them down on  paper and  you know hopefully you do hopefully you  persist  but  it's a tough gig were there were there  things that you learned when you were  writing you know serial television that  would inform your books later on  uh yeah certainly i mean  one of the the structure of uh song of  ice and fire with his viewpoint things  and  and  cutting between the viewpoints and all  of them ending um essentially with i  don't know cliff hangers or interesting  points is is a structure i learned in  television uh you won't see it in my  earlier novels  but of course working for network  television you have to have act breaks  and depending on the structure show is  it a four four rack show is it 4x plus a  teaser or  5x uh or is it  a teaser and a tag you know what have  you but  you always have to go out on an act  break before you go to the commercial  and it can be a cliffhanger that's a  good act break it doesn't have to be it  can be just a a twist point or a new  revelation or a piece of information or  um you know something is resolved but  you know it's it's if you watch law and  order it's always  moments you know where right before the  commercial  oh my god something has changed here um  and uh the idea is to bring you back  after the commercial of course but it's  a good way to end a chapter or or an act  and sometimes it's pretty hard when  you're designing your story to come up  with those act breaks but uh you know  that's why you get the big books and it  certainly works uh  in the books too i mean uh it's  keeps people reading you know they reach  the end of the chapter and and they want  to know what happens  to that character because they're  engrossed in tyrion but gosh there's not  another tyrion chapter for a while yet  first they have to redisar your chapter  or this jon snow chapter before they  finally get back to tyrion and those end  and by the time they finish the arya  chapter there's a kind of an act break  there too and that hopefully draws them  back for the next one so yes  you
 

